=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The engineer's escalation ticket alleged an OSPF stub area (Area 4 behind R2) was "stuck in a route-summarization loop," causing packets to loop or drop. Thorough investigation at every layer finds no such fault.

**Affected component:** None — the network is operating correctly.

**Mechanism (why no fault exists):** Area 4 (`0.0.0.4`) is declared `area 4 stub` consistently on both its ABR (r2) and its sole internal router (r10). Both hold a Full OSPF adjacency over the 21.0.0.0/8 link. No `area range` summarization command is configured on r2 or any other router — the premise of a summarization loop has no configuration basis.

**Localization:** R2 and R10 are healthy. The two OSPF anomalies surfaced by `ospf_snapshot` — r8's "orphaned" area `0.0.0.2` and r13/r12 having no OSPF config — are both by design: r8 is the sole router in area 2 (its own loopbacks + a stub segment), connected to the backbone via a working virtual link through area 1 (r4↔r8 VLINK0, Full/DROther); r12 and r13 are EIGRP-domain routers; r11 is the EIGRP-to-OSPF redistribution boundary, correctly redistributing EIGRP and connected routes into OSPF area 3.

**Root cause:** No fault exists. The engineer misidentified a correctly operating stub area and a non-existent summarization loop; end-to-end reachability between pc0 and pc12 is 0% loss in both directions with sub-millisecond RTTs.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. If the engineer wishes to validate stub area behavior, `vtysh -c 'show ip ospf database summary'` on r10 will confirm the Type-3 default (0.0.0.0/0) injected by r2.
=== END ===